{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"5d47532b92fef2d9","name":"card_event_surveil","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/NavalWarfare/events/card_event_surveil","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SURVEIL","text":"Choose a card in hand. Convert it into target unit.","flavorText":"During WWII, Soviet reconnaissance tactics focused on close-range observation and aggressive patrolling. The Red Army often lacked the advanced aerial surveillance tools used by the Western Allies, so they relied heavily on ground-based scouting. These missions were typically carried out by Razvedchiki scouts, specialized reconnaissance troops trained to operate in small teams. These teams moved silently and used camouflage to blend into the terrain. Their primary tasks were to locate enemy positions, monitor troop movements, and report back quickly. They often crossed into enemy territory at night, sometimes crawling for hours to avoid detection. Capturing enemy soldiers for interrogation was a common objective. The Soviets also used armored scout cars such as the BA-64 and light tanks such as the T-70 for mobile reconnaissance, especially during fast-moving operations. On the aerial side, aircraft such as the Polikarpov Po-2 and the Yak-9R were used for photo-reconnaissance, though they had limited range and were vulnerable to enemy fighters. Despite these challenges, Soviet recon units provided essential intelligence that shaped frontline tactics and helped direct artillery fire with greater accuracy.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_surveil","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_surveil_title","SourceString":"SURVEIL","LocalizedString":"SURVEIL"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_surveil_text","SourceString":"Choose a card in hand. Convert it into target unit.","LocalizedString":"Choose a card in hand. Convert it into target unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_surveil_history","SourceString":"During WWII, Soviet reconnaissance tactics focused on close-range observation and aggressive patrolling. The Red Army often lacked the advanced aerial surveillance tools used by the Western Allies, so they relied heavily on ground-based scouting. These missions were typically carried out by Razvedchiki scouts, specialized reconnaissance troops trained to operate in small teams. These teams moved silently and used camouflage to blend into the terrain. Their primary tasks were to locate enemy positions, monitor troop movements, and report back quickly. They often crossed into enemy territory at night, sometimes crawling for hours to avoid detection. Capturing enemy soldiers for interrogation was a common objective. The Soviets also used armored scout cars such as the BA-64 and light tanks such as the T-70 for mobile reconnaissance, especially during fast-moving operations. On the aerial side, aircraft such as the Polikarpov Po-2 and the Yak-9R were used for photo-reconnaissance, though they had limited range and were vulnerable to enemy fighters. Despite these challenges, Soviet recon units provided essential intelligence that shaped frontline tactics and helped direct artillery fire with greater accuracy.","LocalizedString":"During WWII, Soviet reconnaissance tactics focused on close-range observation and aggressive patrolling. The Red Army often lacked the advanced aerial surveillance tools used by the Western Allies, so they relied heavily on ground-based scouting. These missions were typically carried out by Razvedchiki scouts, specialized reconnaissance troops trained to operate in small teams. These teams moved silently and used camouflage to blend into the terrain. Their primary tasks were to locate enemy positions, monitor troop movements, and report back quickly. They often crossed into enemy territory at night, sometimes crawling for hours to avoid detection. Capturing enemy soldiers for interrogation was a common objective. The Soviets also used armored scout cars such as the BA-64 and light tanks such as the T-70 for mobile reconnaissance, especially during fast-moving operations. On the aerial side, aircraft such as the Polikarpov Po-2 and the Yak-9R were used for photo-reconnaissance, though they had limited range and were vulnerable to enemy fighters. Despite these challenges, Soviet recon units provided essential intelligence that shaped frontline tactics and helped direct artillery fire with greater accuracy."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"waitWithOnOtherCardPlayedUntilHandTargetSelected":true,"selectHandCardNotifyText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"ED06D88D47E36E1AD36F4BB6D611CDA3","SourceString":"Choose a card in hand","LocalizedString":"Choose a card in hand"},"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Convert"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'October_Red'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/October_Red.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/NavalWarfare/t_surveil.t_surveil","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Soviet WWII Poster","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SURVEIL.SURVEIL","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":382.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_surveil_photo","SourceString":"Red Army reconnaissance troops in BA-64 scout cars in 1942.","LocalizedString":"Red Army reconnaissance troops in BA-64 scout cars in 1942."},"isDirectDamage":true},"variables":[{"name":"unitTargeted","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_surveil_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"r